  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "\\text{Express the lattice-point counting inputs of the optimal packings in } \\mathbb{R}^8\\ (E_8)\\ \\text{and } \\mathbb{R}^{24}\\ (\\Lambda_{24}) \\text{ within Minkowski's geometry-of-numbers framework, isolating the analytic ingredients (modular forms, LP bounds) as hypotheses.}",
    "plain": "Minkowski's fundamental theorem bounds lattice points in symmetric convex bodies. The Viazovska (E8, 2016) and CKMRV (Leech, 2017) sphere-packing optimality proofs use modular-form magic functions plus the Cohn–Elkies LP bound, well beyond Minkowski. But the shared object is lattice-point / convex-body counting. We ask how much of the E8/Leech setup fits the Minkowski framework and precisely which parts genuinely need the analytic inputs.",
    "whyMatters": [
      "It clarifies the boundary between elementary geometry of numbers and the modern analytic sphere-packing breakthroughs.",
      "A Lean scaffold deriving density from the Cohn–Elkies LP bound (stated as a hypothesis) would be a valuable, honestly-scoped contribution reusing Mathlib's Minkowski theorem."
    ]
  },
